Path demonstrated deterministically, at the suite's own spawn boundary:
1. Tool authors a legitimate `closed` event; chain_pending:<id>=<authored sha>.
2. pushChain verifies proven(knownTip)=<authored sha> — check PASSES.
3. `git push <url> refs/box-bounty/<id>:refs/box-bounty/<id>` resolves the
   ref NAME inside the push — the hook swaps the local ref to a forged,
   never-scanned commit (a fast-forward child of the remote tip) at the
   instant push is spawned.
4. The forged sha is what reaches the bare remote. chain_pushed:<id> then
   records the forged sha — the laundering becomes permanent provenance.
5. retryPending uses the same pushOnce refspec — the gap exists on every
   push, not only the first.

Why it matters: the whole correction binds publication to "the exact tip
this machine is entitled to publish". The refspec source is the mutable
ref, not the verified sha — check and use are different reads. The fix is
atomic and cheap: push `<provenSha>:refs/box-bounty/<id>` so the verified
commit itself is the push source.
